[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 7:47] Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. What do those figures tell you about where the sixteen points came from?

[candidate · 9:17] Very clean — this resolves it. Let me run it.

Beans were 25% of COGS two years ago, so that's 25 points of revenue sitting in beans. Bean costs are up 40%, so 25 times 1.4 gives you 35 points — that's a 10-point hit to margin right there from coffee alone.

Now the other inputs — dairy, packaging, food. They're the remaining 75% of COGS, so 75 points, and they're up 37.5%. But I only have 16 points of total deterioration to work with and beans already took 10, so the other inputs can only be contributing about 6. That implies they're a much smaller slice of the economics than the 75% headline suggests — which makes sense, because dairy and packaging are pennies per cup against the bean cost in specialty coffee.

So the answer: roughly 10 of the 16 points are green coffee inflation, about 6 points are dairy, packaging and food, and the remaining 2 points of the total 18 are overhead creep. Beans are the single largest driver, roughly 60% of the margin destruction.

And critically — this is exogenous. Commodity coffee has run hard on Brazilian and Vietnamese supply shocks. Brew & Bean didn't get worse at operating; they got caught unhedged with flat menu pricing while their primary input repriced 40%. Two years of zero price increases against a 40% input move is the actual failure here. That's a pricing-discipline problem, not a cost-control problem.
