[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:53] I don't have information on their hedging position. Exhibit A is available if you want to check your cost walk against the full picture. First, though, what would you say the root cause is, given the split you just walked through?

[candidate · 12:23] Root cause, stated plainly: Brew & Bean has no pricing mechanism. They absorbed a roughly 38 to 40% input shock across their entire basket with zero menu movement in 24 months, and the exhibit shows it's not a one-off — it's a steady eight-point-a-year bleed, 42 to 50 to 58. That's a structural pass-through failure, not a commodity event.

And the volume picture makes it worse. Revenue up 15% on flat pricing and flat footprint means 15% more cups, and every incremental cup is now sold at a negative contribution relative to two years ago. They're growing themselves into the ground.

Let me size the fix. Beans are 25% of COGS, so take price up 25% and you'd fully neutralize the bean exposure — that alone recovers a double-digit margin point. More practically, I'd model a 12% menu increase. On $480M that's roughly $57.6M of incremental revenue, and because COGS is variable it flows almost entirely to the bottom line, so you'd add about 12 points of margin and land near 18% — most of the way back to the 24% starting point.

The elasticity risk is real but manageable: specialty coffee is habitual, daily-ritual consumption, and with traffic up 15% they've demonstrated latent demand headroom. If you lose 3 or 4% of transactions on a 12% price move, you're still massively net positive.

Supporting levers: hedge green coffee twelve to eighteen months forward, renegotiate dairy and packaging on the larger volume base, and attack the two points of overhead creep.
