[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 12:25] I'll take your recommendation as stated. Beyond the price increase, hedging, and procurement levers you listed, what else could Brew & Bean do to protect margin or grow profit?

[candidate · 13:55] Happy to broaden it. I'd bucket the remaining levers into four areas.

Mix and menu engineering. Shift the basket toward low-bean-content, high-margin items — cold brew uses more beans, so I'd actually steer away from it, and push food attach, pastries, breakfast sandwiches, where the input basket is unrelated to coffee. Food attach in this segment typically sits around 18% of tickets; getting that to 30% adds real dollars with no bean exposure.

Portfolio and format. Introduce a value tier using a cheaper blend to protect traffic at the low end while premium pricing carries the top. That lets you take the 12% headline increase without alienating price-sensitive dayparts.

Operational. Waste and shrink is buried inside that COGS line — in coffee, spoilage and over-pour typically run 3 to 5% of COGS, so tightening to 2% is a point or more of margin at essentially zero capex. Portion control on milk steaming alone is meaningful.

Channel. Packaged beans and subscription at-home, which carries far better margin and converts a 40% input shock into a pass-through you control directly, plus loyalty to defend the volume.

The two points of overhead creep I'd also attack — on $480M that's $9.6M, and with flat footprint there's no reason overhead grew at all. That's pure slippage and it should be recoverable inside a year.
