[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 13:57] Understood. Pick the two or three of those ideas you'd put in front of the CEO first, and what criteria are you using to rank them?

[candidate · 15:27] I'd rank on three criteria: size of margin impact, speed to realization, and risk to traffic. That last one matters because volume is the one thing currently working.

Top of the list, waste and shrink. Waste runs about 4% of COGS, and COGS is 58% of revenue — so call it 4 points of revenue. On $480M that's roughly $19M, and halving it gives you two full points of margin inside a quarter. No capex, no customer-facing risk, and the CEO can start Monday. That's the easiest point of margin in the business.

Second, food attach. Moving attach from 18% to 30% of tickets puts incremental revenue through a cost basket that has nothing to do with green coffee, so it structurally de-risks the P&L against the next bean shock. Six to nine months, mostly an operations and merchandising lift.

Third, the two points of overhead creep — $9.6M, flat footprint, no justification for it having grown at all. Lowest customer risk of anything on the list.

I'd sequence the price increase alongside these, not after, because it's still the single biggest lever at roughly 12 points. But these three are what I'd lead with because they're defensible, fast, and none of them touch traffic.
